{{infobox shinto shrine|religious_affiliation=[[Shinto]]|image=Koganeyama-jinja Haiden.jpg|map_type=Japan|coordinates={{coordinates|38|17|52.9|N|141|33|21.6|E}}|location=5 Kanagasaki, Ayukawahama, Ishinomaki City, Miyagi Prefecture|locale=5 Kanagasaki, Ayukawahama, Ishinomaki City, Miyagi Prefecture|deity=- Kanayamahiko-no-Kami - Kanayamahime-no-Kami - Heavenly Myriad Deities - Earthly Myriad Deities|festivals=September 25|festival=September 25|consecration_year=Year 750 (during the Tenpyo-shoho era)|type=Listed Minor Shrine, Former Prefectural Shrine, [[Beppyo Shrine]]|architecture_type=Three-bay Shrine Architecture with Copper Roofing|architecture_style=Three-bay Shrine Architecture with Copper Roofing}}
Koganeyama Shrine (黄金山神社) is a [[Shinto shrine]] located in [[Japan]]. It is a [[Beppyo shrine]], or a shrine that is particularly notable in a certain way with a significant history to it.<ref>{{Cite web|title=別表神社とは？御朱印めぐりに参考になる「別表神社一覧」とマップ {{!}} 開運戦隊ゴシュインジャー|url=https://jinja-gosyuin.com/beppyou/|access-date=2023-04-02|website=jinja-gosyuin.com}}</ref>
It is located on Mount Koganeyama, an isolated island in [[Pacific Ocean|the Pacific Ocean]] opposite the southeastern tip of the {{ill|10=ja|11=牡鹿半島|Oshika Peninsula|fr|Péninsule d'Oshika|en}} in [[Ishinomaki]] [[Miyagi Prefecture|City]], {{ill|10=ja|11=金華山_(宮城県)|Miyagi Prefecture}}. Its [[Modern system of Ranked Shinto shrines|former status]] was that of [[Modern system of Ranked Shinto shrines|a prefectural shrine]], but after the war [[Beppyo shrine|it became a special listed shrine]] under [[Association of Shinto Shrines|the Association of Shinto Shrines]] .
It is located facing west on the middle of the western slope of Mt. Kinka, but since the entire island of Mt. Kinka is considered a sacred area, the shrine and Mt. Kinka (island) are sometimes used almost synonymously, and it is also commonly known as Koganeyama Shrine or Koganeyama Koganeyama Shrine . The temple is bustling with worshippers and tourists praying for worldly benefits such as prosperity in business and better luck, but before modern times it was a {{ill|10=ja|11=真言宗|Shingon Buddhism|lt=Shingon sect|en}} {{ill|10=ja|11=寺院|temple}} called Koganeyama Daikin-ji, which had {{ill|10=ja|11=女人禁制|lt=no women allowed|2=en|Nyonin Kinsei}} and dedicated to [[Benzaiten]] (Benten). It is counted as one of the " Five Benten Shrines " in [[Japan]] along with [[Itsukushima Shrine]] in [[Hiroshima Prefecture]] <ref>他の五弁天は[[近江国|江州]]竹生島（[[都久夫須麻神社]]）、[[相模国|相州]]江ノ島（[[江島神社]]）、[[Yamato Province |和州]]天川（[[天河大弁財天社]]）であった（『[[和漢三才図会]]』巻第79）。</ref>, and is counted as one of the " Three Holy Sites of [[Tōhoku region|Tōhoku]], " alongside the [[Three Mountains of Dewa]] (Dewa Sanzan) in [[Yamagata Prefecture]] and {{ill|10=ja|11=恐山|Mount Osore|lt=Osorezan|en|4=User:Immanuelle/draft}} in [[Aomori Prefecture]] . == deity == Before the [[Meiji period]], Benzaiten was enshrined, and in the early modern period her divine virtues of bestowing good fortune were widely publicized, and she became renowned for her remarkable divine powers in bringing worldly benefits such as good luck and prosperity, especially in bringing about financial and wealthy fortune. Her worship has continued even after she was redesignated as the current enshrined deity in the Meiji period. == History == Koganeyama Shrine was established in the second year of the Tenpyo Shoho era (750 AD) near the site of Japan's first gold mine on Mount Kinka, vital for funding national projects like the gilding of the Todaiji Temple's Great Buddha. Initially, the shrine was dedicated to the gods of gold mining, Kanayamabiko and Kanayamabime, and later became associated with Benzaiten, embodying prosperity and good fortune.
Legend ties the shrine to celestial origins, suggesting Benzaiten was a celestial maiden who inspired the shrine's location. Over centuries, the shrine's influence grew, supported by the Fujiwara clan among others, and became a center for Shugendo practitioners, linking spiritual practices with the region's rich gold deposits.
Despite various reconstructions due to fire and war, the shrine's cultural and spiritual significance persisted. Following the Meiji Restoration, it transitioned from a Buddhist temple to a Shinto shrine, reflecting broader religious reforms of the period. The shrine buildings, repeatedly damaged and rebuilt, today continue to serve as a site of pilgrimage and cultural heritage, closely tied to the community and the natural landscape of the region.
